Sorry for being late, but I have made some tests -after hours-
in hope to help and having maximum information about this problem.

I tried to point out exactly the problem, my preceding description is 
a consequence of what exactly happen.
No need of isakmpd, nor Cisco on some side.

Resume :
hostA -- GW1 <==> GW2 -- hostB.

hostA (10.0.0.2), GW1 (10.0.0.1, 192.168.0.1), GW2 (192.168.0.2, 11.0.0.1),
      hostB (11.0.0.2)
  
On GW1 :
#setkey -f 
flush;
spdflush;
add 192.168.1.1 192.168.1.2 esp 1000 -m tunnel -E des-cbc "12345678";
spdadd 10.0.0.0/24 11.0.0.0/24 any -P out ipsec
esp/tunnel/192.168.1.1-192.168.1.2/require;

On GW2 : (only need to have SPI to decrypt packets coming from GW1) 
#!/usr/local/sbin/setkey -f
flush;
spdflush;
add -n 192.168.1.1 192.168.1.2 esp 1000 -m tunnel -E des-cbc "12345678";

>From hostA (which have a route to 11.0.0.2 via gw 10.0.0.1, I try to ping 
hostB. Packets are correctly encrypted on GW1, transmitted to GW2, and the
problem is here : GW2 does not forward decrypted packets to hostB.
(of course net/ipv4/conf/all/forwarding = 1 on GW2).

I have tried the following kernels on GW2 (all with same conf, see attachements):

- 2.6.4, woody filesystem: no bug 
(I have compiled setkey myself for woody: ipsec-tools-0.6.1)
- 2.6.6, woody filesystem: no bug
- 2.6.8.1, woody and sarge filesystems: no bug
- 2.6.9 , woody : no bug (work), sarge filesystem: bug (not working) !!
- 2.6.10, 2.6.12, 2.6.14, 2.6.16.9, 2.6.17.1: woody AND sarge filesystems: bug.  

For kernel 2.6.9, the situation is very surprising. Same kernel, different
glibc... 

If I have some time I will look patches between 2.6.8.1 and 2.6.9 about
networking... But I had never look into kernel source code, so I will certainly 
not be efficient... If you can give me some hints where to look...

Note : I have made a lot of tests with UML (and tuntap), 
but I have also verify the same things with "real" ethernet with kernels 2.6.8,
2.6.14 and 2.6.16.9.   

Regards,

Raphael Astier.
